cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Power and Cell: programmable, self-dividing current cells, their energy exchange and atom-level control"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "counfield"
    website: "https://counfield.com/"
repository-code: "https://github.com/berkmec/power-and-cell"
url: "https://counfield.com/"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-23"
abstract: >-
  Pre-registered model and simulation study of self-dividing current filaments ("cells") in a wide, ballasted n-channel
  transistor of a standard open 130 nm CMOS process (reduced cryogenic model built from a compact-model card and ngspice).
  A cell splits when the drain voltage at its centre reaches the lowest voltage at which the latched state exists, which
  gives a parameter-free split law validated against pre-registered simulations. The cells can be programmed (birth site,
  number, regeneration, simultaneous splitting), exchange power with their neighbours, and are extended to atom-level
  control. The model is closed against published measurements; failed predictions and negative results are reported.
keywords:
  - current filaments
  - programmable cells
  - pattern formation
  - self-replication
  - energy recycling
  - active droplets
  - reaction-diffusion
  - open-source PDK
